INSERT INTO documents (document_id, feature_id, kind, seq, title, frozen, frozen_date, body, render_path, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (58, 31, 'doc', 7, 'CONFORMANCE: Sprints v2.0 build — shard B (REV2)', 0, NULL, '# CONFORMANCE: Sprints v2.0 build — shard B (REV2)

- **Sprint:** doc #51 (SPRINT: Sprints v2.0 build), close-out conformance, unit C shard B (task msg #611).
- **Spec:** doc #46 "Sprints v2.0 — Collaboration Loop", current revision (REV8 banner chain, REV4 QAQC PASS + REV5–REV8 recorded mid-Sprint edits).
- **Judged artifact:** `~/Repos/subfloor` main @ **cc33349** (all 10 units merged). Judged against the code on main — never the diffs or the message trail.
- **Shard scope (second half of the spec body):** Dev and Review Loop · Sprint Skills · Liveness Monitoring · Pause and Recovery · Deterministic Capture / report compiler · Sprint Close and Conformance · Core-Systems gates 6–10 · Adversarial Acceptance · shell-facing command surface incl. R6 lifecycle/register additions · Stage-10 live proof.
- **Reviewer:** REV2 (shell #8). Method: seven parallel adversarial evidence passes (one per area), every load-bearing claim then re-verified by REV2 against the code; all area test files executed green (full suite 1386 passed, 1 skipped).
- **Ratified judgement calls applied:** rulings R2–R6, decisions #38/#42/#44, banked REV1 u9-L2 (replacement-Planner authority) — these distinguish intentional from silent deviation.

## Verdict: FAIL — 2 Major, 9 Medium, 14 Low

The loop wiring, liveness core, pause/resume machinery, skills, command surface, and the acceptance sweep are substantially as-specced and well-tested. The Sprint Close area has two unimplemented spec requirements (final report, follow-up disposition), and nine Medium defects cluster around recovery dead-ends, evidence gaps, and untested authority paths. Per the spec, conformance findings are not fixed inside the Sprint — they are recorded here as follow-ups for FnB disposition.

## Findings

### Major

- **C-M1 — No write path for the final sprint report; `completed` requires neither conformance nor a final report.** Spec: Lifecycle `completed` row ("Conformance and the final report are recorded") and Sprint Close and Conformance ("The Planner then closes the Sprint with a full sprint report covering the conformance outcome, judgments, issues, mid-Sprint spec edits, and the follow-up list"). On main, `sprint_reports` has exactly three writers — pause (`sprint_domain.py:536`), abort (`sprint_domain.py:365`), conformance (`sprint_close.py:68`) — and no writer for `report_kind=''final''` exists anywhere in non-test code. `sc sprint complete` accepts only `--reason/--outcome` (`sprint_cli.py:284-288`), and the transition requires only `terminal_outcome` (`sprint_domain.py:190-193`). The `sprint_close` skill instructs "commit the final or abort report before the lifecycle transition" against a command that does not exist. A Sprint can reach `completed` with zero conformance and zero final report; the spec''s terminal-state definition is both unimplementable and unenforced. **Verdict class: unimplemented.**
- **C-M2 — FnB cannot disposition conformance follow-ups.** Spec: "recorded as follow-ups for FnB review… The Sprint ships; the FnB dispositions the follow-ups." There is no `UPDATE sprint_followups` path anywhere in the engine — no API endpoint, no CLI verb. The schema anticipates disposition (0150''s `accepted/resolved/dismissed` + resolution CHECK, and an immutability trigger that carves out disposition columns), but nothing can move a follow-up out of `pending`; the report packet reads `pending`/`accepted` as unresolved forever. **Verdict class: unimplemented.**

### Medium

- **C-m1 — Post-approval head move wedges the unit in `merge_ready` (deviated-silently).** After an approved review, if the PR head moves, `authorize_merge` rejects the stale approval (`sprint_review_loop.py:247-251`), but `request_review` refuses `merge_ready` (`:104-107`) and `record_review` requires `in_review` (`:175-178`). No re-review or un-wedge path exists; the merge grant''s staleness semantics ("waits for the watcher''s next green wake") cover regressed checks at the same head only. Verified empirically against the test fixture; no test covers the scenario.
- **C-m2 — Same-state head movement is invisible to the transition log (deviated-silently).** `observe_in_transaction` dedupes on `normalized_state` only (`sprint_pr_watcher.py:405-407`): green→green at a new head records no transition, fires no wake, and leaves `observed_head_sha` stale. `request_review`/`record_review` staleness checks compare against this log, so a review can be requested and approved against a superseded head — the merge gate catches it later, and then C-m1 wedges the unit.
- **C-m3 — Wedged-but-alive worker never escalates (partially deviated-intentionally).** Post-nudge Planner escalation requires `not snapshot.supporting` (`sprint_liveness.py:726-733`), and `process.present` is stamped `observed_at=now` on every evaluation (`:450-460`), so a live-but-hung harness process defers escalation forever (test asserts no escalation even at +40 min). Decision #42 (ratified) blesses suppression "while it positively shows healthy work" — but bare process presence is not positive evidence of healthy work, so the mechanism is broader than the ratification. The spec''s policy text is unconditional ("continued silence beyond a further ten-minute window creates one active Planner escalation"); the carve-out needs either a ceiling in code or a spec write-back.
- **C-m4 — Git commits are not consumed as strong liveness evidence (deviated-silently).** The spec''s strong-evidence list includes "Git commits and registered PR transitions". Registered PR transitions are consumed (`sprint_liveness.py:353-370`); git commits are not — only a non-commit-specific `worktree.observed` supporting signal, refreshed at run completion (`:415-436`; `conversation_git_targets.py:178-188`).
- **C-m5 — Terminally-failed wakes are stranded across resume, and invisible in reconciliation (deviated-silently).** `record_wake_failure` sets the wake `failed` (`sprint_domain.py:447-458`); nothing ever resets it (`claim_next` takes only `pending`/expired-`delivering`, `sprint_message_delivery.py:487-489`); resume evidence lists only pending/delivering wakes (`sprint_domain.py:847-854`). The canonical auto-pause scenario (`wake_delivery_exhausted`) resumes with the very assignment that caused the pause undelivered and unsurfaced — recovery depends on an unrelated future wake or a liveness nudge. No test exercises resume after a `wake_delivery_exhausted` pause.
- **C-m6 — Bound-spec edit guard is resume-granular only (deviated-silently).** Spec: a bound governing spec "may be edited only by its owning Planner or the FnB; the system records any other write as drift evidence and notifies the Planner the same way (a policy guard with detection, not a write lock)". On main, `patch_document` checks only `frozen` (`server.py:1109-1120`) — any shell may edit a bound spec mid-Sprint with no write-time detection, no actor recorded, no notification; drift is computed only at resume by sha compare (`sprint_domain.py:771-786`), which is blind to edit-then-revert and cannot name the writer. An armed Sprint that never pauses never detects mid-Sprint edits at all (they surface only as hash drift in the close packet).
- **C-m7 — The evidence packet''s `pause_and_recovery` section omits resume/reconciliation (deviated-silently).** The section filters events on markers `pause/resume/recover/interrupt/drift` (`sprint_close.py:134-141`), but resume emits `lifecycle.reconciled` and `lifecycle.armed` (`sprint_domain.py:288-323`) — the single richest recovery evidence (reconciliation payload incl. spec drift and anomalies) matches no marker and never appears in the section or in `anomalies`.
- **C-m8 — "FnB drives close" positive path is untested; the acceptance manifest overclaims (deviated-silently).** The manifest maps "FnB authority can compile evidence when the Planner is unavailable" (`tests/fixtures/sprint_v2_acceptance.json:80-82`) to `test_only_planner_or_admin_compiles_and_participants_read_timeline`, whose denied caller is a dev-flavored shell — the admin branch of `_require_close_authority` (`sprint_close.py:410-414`) is never positively exercised, and FnB `complete` is untested. The mechanism exists (R4/banked u9-L2 below); the evidence does not.
- **C-m9 — Report-only/no-code work units cannot complete (unimplemented surface).** Spec: "A work unit explicitly planned as report-only or no-code may complete with a durable result rather than a PR." `SprintWorkUnitStore.complete` (`sprint_domain.py:1397`) implements it but has zero production callers — no endpoint, no CLI verb; the `cancelled` disposition is likewise unwritable. In a live Sprint the only route to `completed` is merge observation, so a Sprint containing a no-code unit can never fully terminate (unresolved units surface in the packet but nothing can resolve them).

### Low

- **C-L1 (loop):** TOCTOU between `authorize_merge`''s live re-read and the actual merge click; spec-silent on how tight "at merge time" must be. The skill mitigates (exact returned head, merge immediately).
- **C-L2 (loop/close):** No general judgment-recording API — `sprint_judgments` is written only by `request_review`/`record_review`; Planner scope calls, mid-Sprint spec-edit rationale, and Developer mid-work judgment calls (which the spec and skills require to be recorded) have no surface.
- **C-L3 (loop):** `request_review` trusts the transition log for greenness — a legitimate handoff is rejected if the watcher hasn''t pulsed since checks went green.
- **C-L4 (liveness):** No "later threshold" re-escalation — silence escalations never repeat within an episode (spec: "deduplicated until new evidence arrives or a later escalation threshold is reached").
- **C-L5 (liveness):** Expired-lease escalation is indirect and untested — the monitor never reads `lease_expires_at`; only broker reconciliation proving the run dead escalates (R4-consistent, but the spec''s escalate-immediately list item has no direct signal).
- **C-L6 (liveness):** The no-fallback escalation path (`route=''unavailable''`, `pause_option`, inactive message) is untested; the durable-but-inactive escalation is never delivered later.
- **C-L7 (liveness):** Late acceptance on a terminal work unit leaks an expectation (acceptance trigger 0149:74-89 doesn''t exclude terminal units) → spurious nudge/escalation; backfilled expectations get no upgrade-time grace (0149:63).
- **C-L8 (recovery):** Post-commit notification failure raises a 500 for an already-committed pause/abort, and can skip in-session interrupt signaling (`sprint_domain.py:1032-1036` unwrapped).
- **C-L9 (recovery/auth):** `_sprint_actor` ignores `is_deleted` (`server.py:1898-1917`) — a deleted shell with a valid token retains pause/resume/abort authority.
- **C-L10 (recovery/ops):** `SprintRecoveryCoordinator.recover_on_startup` is test-only — production restart uses a different path (`sprint_runtime.py:219`, `sprint_pr_watcher.py:268`); plus dead divergent `_start_runtime_services` (`server.py:3821-3828`). Divergence risk, guarded only by a source-string test.
- **C-L11 (recovery):** Resume details: replay does wasted GitHub egress on already-armed; liveness `next_evaluation_at` is not shifted for pause duration (thundering herd on resume); dispatch proceeds to capacity-anomalous/deleted participants; one participant missing a persistent conversation hard-blocks the whole resume via `SprintConversationError` (loud, arguably against "informs, never silently blocks").
- **C-L12 (close):** Abort report''s `recovery_disposition` is hardcoded `""` (`sprint_domain.py:691`) — the schema promises a field no actor can fill; `record_conformance` checks armed before idempotent replay, so a late retry after terminal transition errors instead of replaying its receipt; follow-up severity is free text (1–32 chars, no enum).
- **C-L13 (capture):** Deterministic Capture rows partial — no tool-activity capture surface; work-unit state history events sparse (`active/in_review/fixing/merge_ready` emit no events); no sprint-scoped git/worktree observation capture; packet compile reads with no snapshot isolation.
- **C-L14 (skills/surface/acceptance):** `--merge-grant` is a required `store_true` (declare-time binding; skill prose implies arming-time; a no-grant Sprint is unexpressible); `test_skills_use_only_the_shipped_shell_command_surface` doesn''t check the "only" direction; no decline command exists though `sprint_dev`/`sprint_pln` name declining; `sc` help lists 7 of 14 sprint subcommands; work-unit `blocked` enum value is dead; the acceptance-manifest guard asserts `def <name>` string presence only; the manifest silently narrowed the "browser entry" bullet to messaging-only; green→review-handoff is never causally asserted (proof''s `request_review` is CLI-initiated, not wake-triggered).

## Per-section verdicts (four-way)

- **Dev and Review Loop (steps 1–10): as-specced**, with findings C-m1/C-m2 (deviated-silently) and C-L1/C-L3. Verified: mark-read acceptance with rowcount guard; watcher red/green active wakes resolved to the current conversation at claim time (fix-loop landing proven); fresh linked fix/merge conversations with pointer moves; green-at-merge-time enforced by live re-read outside the txn; merge auto-observation completing units and dispatching downstream atomically; next assignment restoring the persistent lane; Planner passive except closed-without-merge/declines/escalations. **REV7/R3 as-specced** (registration rejects multi-unit sets; per-lane hard-fail). **REV8/R5 as-specced** (closed-without-merge resolves the unit''s review-request expectations without inventing an outcome). Medium+ severity filtering is reviewer discipline by design — no severity field exists, consistent with the spec delegating the rubric to `sprint_rev`.
- **Sprint Skills: as-specced.** All five exist, are substantive (goals/boundaries/evidence/stop conditions), seeded by 0151 with exact flavor grants (byte-match tested), the severity rubric is owned solely by `sprint_rev`, conformance and report procedures live in `sprint_rev`/`sprint_close`, and all 15 skill-referenced commands exist end-to-end in `sprint_cli.py` + the API — the R6 class of defect is not present. Lows in C-L14.
- **Liveness Monitoring: as-specced core** (5-min evaluation of accepted actionable expectations incl. review requests; armed-only; evidence hierarchy; 10-min grace; extension on fresh strong; proven-failure immediate escalation; exactly one nudge per episode; one deduped Planner escalation with evidence + unreadable signals; fallback-only Planner delivery, never re-booting the worker; uncertainty never corrupts unit state; R4 unknown-run posture implemented and tested), with findings C-m3/C-m4 and C-L4–C-L7. Stage 7 gate (fake-clock grace/extension/nudge/escalation/dedup) passes.
- **Pause and Recovery: as-specced core** (atomic pause + reason before report; any participant may pause, Planner/FnB resume, Planner/FnB-only abort incl. prepared-discard with stub report; auto-pause on third wake failure via the same machinery; services stop on paused; interrupt intent persisted and retryable; full retention; active Planner notice; pause-report template; resume reconciles native runs/messages/wakes/units/PRs/capacity/spec eligibility before re-arm; drift informs and never blocks; bound-revision QAQC not re-validated; decision #44 verified — no schema gap from the no-new-migration implementation of u6-L1 + R5), with findings C-m5/C-m6 and C-L8–C-L11. Stage 8 gate passes.
- **Deterministic Capture / report compiler: partial.** Bounded evidence packet with truncation counters, full-history links, and anomaly detail is real and tested (Stage 9 gate passes); but see C-M1 (final report), C-m7 (recovery section), C-L13 (capture rows). `spec_revisions.mid_sprint_edits` reads `spec.*`/`drift` events nothing emits — a dead section, though the information survives via bound-vs-current hash comparison (folded into C-m7''s area, not separately rated).
- **Sprint Close and Conformance: FAIL — see C-M1, C-M2**, plus C-m8. Conformance recording itself is as-specced: reviewer-only, armed-only, findings land as immutable follow-ups and never reopen a fix lane at any severity (tested), cross-Sprint links rejected. Replacement-Planner authority resolves as "originating Planner or FnB only; a replacement Planner gets zero close-out authority" — the banked REV1 u9-L2 clarification is confirmed live and should be written into the spec.
- **Shell-facing command surface incl. R6: as-specced.** All lifecycle verbs (declare/plan-unit/arm/pause/resume/complete/abort), register-pr, request-review/record-review/authorize-merge, dispatch, monitor, record-conformance, compile-report exist as thin authenticated callers over the production stores (zero business logic in the CLI); the authority matrix matches the spec (participant-pause/never-abort, Planner-or-FnB resume/abort, originating-Planner binding, per-role loop verbs, 403/409/404 mapping, cross-role refusals side-effect-free). C-m9 and C-L14 lows aside, R6 is fully delivered.
- **Adversarial Acceptance: 15 of 18 bullets implemented with real behavioral tests**; three partial: browser-entry-and-FnB-messaging (messaging half real, browser half unmapped), green→handoff-when-idle (wake-once real, causal handoff unasserted — C-L14), FnB-drives-close (C-m8). The acceptance bar (durable, visible, attributable, retryable/terminal failure) holds everywhere tested.
- **Stage 10 live proof: deviated-intentionally (R6).** Serial and parallel composition are genuinely proven end-to-end through the real HTTP API + bearer identities + production stores; real GitHub was exercised out-of-band and independently re-verified by REV2 (PR #870 closed-unmerged fixture, PR #871 merged 2026-08-01T00:19:09Z, both against throwaway base `fixture/s2-u10-20260801-0022-base`, branches cleaned up in-unit — R6 boundary honored). The committed proof is hermetic (mock GitHub, DB-enqueued turns), and "real browser conversations" was not literally exercised — R6''s "shell-driven" ruling covers the mode, but the spec/gate vocabulary was never reconciled (candidate clarification below). No durable proof write-up exists; the real-run evidence is ephemeral (message rows, deleted branches).

## Candidate spec clarifications (spec silent, implementation chose)

1. **Replacement-Planner authority on close-out (banked REV1 u9-L2 — confirmed):** only the originating Planner or FnB can compile/complete/abort; a booted replacement Planner has no close-out authority. Spec should state this.
2. **Head-change-after-approval recovery:** whether a moved head re-enters review from `merge_ready` or requires Planner intervention (C-m1).
3. **Supporting-evidence deferral of post-nudge escalation:** decision #42 ratified the policy; the spec''s unconditional escalation text should carry the carve-out — and define what "positively shows healthy work" means (C-m3).
4. **"Coalesces behind the active turn"** is implemented as coalescing into an existing pending wake; there is no in-flight-turn detection.
5. **"Real browser conversations" (Stage 10) vs DB-enqueued prompts** — R6 redefined the proof shell-driven; reconcile the gate text with the architecture.
6. **Who executes the merge:** the engine has no merge-execution path (`GitHubPullRequestReader` is read-only); "merge under the grant" means the Developer shell merges externally and the watcher observes. R6''s "engine production merge path" does not exist — vocabulary never reconciled.
7. **Conformance during pause:** `record_conformance` requires `armed`; spec is silent on whether a paused Sprint may record conformance.
8. **Close-out reviewer onboarding:** participants are fixed at declaration; a Sprint declared without a reviewer can never record conformance. Spec should say the conformance reviewer must be declared up front (or demand an add-participant path).
9. **Completion gate:** the engine allows `completed` with non-terminal units (surfaced as unresolved in the packet); spec should say whether this is deliberate.
10. **Merge-grant binding time:** mandatory at declare via the surface; schema permits disabled. State whether no-grant Sprints are in scope.
11. **FnB dispatch/monitor:** exact originating Planner only, no admin override — if the Planner shell is down, nobody can dispatch/monitor. State whether FnB may.
12. **Follow-up `accepted` disposition** has no defined semantics (packet treats it as unresolved, like `pending`); severity vocabulary is conventional, not enforced.

## Disposition

- Verdict: **FAIL** for shard B — C-M1 and C-M2 are unimplemented spec requirements in the close-out path; 9 Mediums; 14 Lows. All findings are recorded for FnB disposition per the spec''s conformance rule; none was fixed in-Sprint.
- REV1 holds shard A (first half) independently; this verdict covers shard B only.
- Evidence basis: seven area passes + REV2''s direct re-verification of every Major/Medium claim (file:line citations above); area suites green (`test_sprint_review_loop` 11/11, `test_sprint_liveness` 13/13, `test_sprint_recovery` 9/9, `test_sprint_close` 8/8, `test_sprint_skills` 3/3, `test_sprint_cli` 6/6, `test_sprint_work_dispatch` 9/9, `test_sprint_live_proof` + manifest-referenced files 140/140); full suite 1386 passed / 1 skipped @ cc33349.
