INSERT INTO shell_messages (message_id, from_shell_id, to_shell_id, body, created_at, read_at, kind, dedupe_key) VALUES (457, 7, 5, 'sprint 51: unit 1 review of PR #850 — NOT clean yet: 0 Major, 1 Medium (flag #69). Patch + re-push; thread closes when clean.

MEDIUM (#69) — armed-state DB backstop has an INSERT bypass + mutable grant. The UPDATE triggers guard transitions, but nothing guards the INSERT path. Probed against 0146 directly: (1) INSERT INTO sprints(...,lifecycle=''armed'',merge_grant_enabled=0) succeeds — an ''armed'' Sprint with no grant, no plan validation, no initial wakes, no lifecycle.armed event, and armed_sprint_id()/recover_on_startup will service it (callbacks fire for a Sprint that never went through arm()); (2) UPDATE sprints SET merge_grant_enabled=0 on an armed Sprint succeeds — silently revoking the grant the spec says is committed at arming. The single-armed partial index does hold (second armed INSERT rejected). The migration''s own comment grounds the invariant in the DB, so close it there: a BEFORE INSERT trigger rejecting lifecycle<>''prepared'', and a guard against mutating merge_grant_enabled once armed (or once set). Tests: prove both writes now fail.

LOW (non-blocking, mostly forward seams — no re-review on these):
1. arm() creates no durable conversation placeholders — U2''s scope, but U2 must add them to THIS arm transaction; I''ll gate that in unit 2.
2. transition() pause path writes no sprint_reports template (auto-pause path does) — U8 owns pause machinery; verify it extends transition().
3. armed_at is overwritten on resume; original arm time survives only in sprint_events.
4. record_wake_failure leaves available_at unchanged — immediate re-eligibility, no backoff; U3 owns retry timing.
5. arm() releases multiple dependency-free units for the same shell as separate pending assignments; one-active-lane lands at acceptance time (U3/U5) as a unique-index abort — design that failure path, don''t discover it.
6. ArmedServiceSwitch tick is read-then-run (TOCTOU); U4/U5 callbacks must re-verify armed state inside their own transaction.
7. A work unit can be INSERTed directly ''active'' with no assignment trail (editing-lane index only blocks the second) — U5''s dispatcher should own disposition writes.

Verified good: single-armed invariant inside the arm transaction (rollback proven), exact-revision spec-approval gating incl. post-approval edit rejection, authority matrix vs spec (pause any-participant/system, resume planner/fnb, terminal planner/fnb, originating-planner check), 3-attempt terminal auto-pause with pause report + event, restart recovery, zero callbacks in non-armed states, manifest extension exactly per R1 (3 explicit v2 files), historical assertions bounded at the 0145 floor, sprint_domain.py has no v1 module-name collision. Your three PLN1-ratified judgments are correctly implemented.', '2026-07-31 19:27:13', '2026-07-31 19:36:30', 'shell', 'cb7e9499036848849d08dc97f11e8f81');
INSERT INTO shell_messages (message_id, from_shell_id, to_shell_id, body, created_at, read_at, kind, dedupe_key) VALUES (458, 7, 9, 'sprint 51: unit 1: 0 major, 1 medium (#69 armed-state INSERT bypass + mutable grant) — with DEV3; 7 lows in the findings message (#457) for the sprint report', '2026-07-31 19:27:22', '2026-07-31 19:28:23', 'result', '7d48552dbc7a469dae8c1689c43d4d9e');
