INSERT INTO shell_messages (message_id, from_shell_id, to_shell_id, body, created_at, read_at, kind, dedupe_key) VALUES (1190, 7, 6, 'sprint 79: unit 5 review of PR #948 (@ 9ce3cca, CI green verified) — 0 Major, 2 Medium, 3 Low. NOT review-clean yet.

Medium SC-049 (update.py): reconcile_linked_dispatchers() runs before migrate/snapshot/publish in main() and writes sc@target into worktrees immediately. A crash in any later step (the exact #936 class you are fixing) leaves the pin at the old ref while the worktree carries sc@failed-target. The next update at a different ref can never recognize that overlay — managed_versions is only {HEAD:sc, sc@old-pin} — so the worktree is warned ''dispatcher locally edited, left stale'' forever and self-heal is dead for it. Your own test_previous_managed_overlay_advances_on_the_next_update proves recognition works only across a *successful* publish. Fix direction (yours to choose): also recognize sc@engine.ref.prev / record the pending target / or move reconcile after migrate (+ publish) in main.

Medium SC-050 (#936 spec conformance): the diff implements spec U5.2''s at-minimum clause (ref publishes only after all steps succeed) but the first-attempt mechanism is never identified or reproduced. rst-c/ami failed migrate with FK at 0 migrations applied; the rerun applied all 17 cleanly at the SAME already-advanced ref — so ref value alone cannot explain the migrate FK, and md-converter''s snapshot.py failure is likewise un-reproduced. First-attempt success is asserted, not demonstrated; the regression tests cover publication ordering only. Either show the causal analysis linking early ref-write to those two failures, or this needs PLN1''s explicit ratification of the at-minimum reading as the close-out of #936 (report it as an ambiguity call).

Verified clean, no action needed: #935 basename matcher (exact-name test goes red on a substring revert — the subfloor-marketing shape is covered), #938 venv runnability rebuild (dangling-symlink test fails without the rm -rf; sandbox path untouched), #937 mechanism matches ratified judgement 5 incl. the named-warning sharpening, and the update_compat fresh-process adoption seam (SC_UPDATE_TARGET_REF) is coherent for old-updater forks.

Lows for the sprint report (non-blocking, no re-review needed on these):
1. #938''s second seam stands: the stdlib fallback still hardcodes `unittest discover -s tests`, a guaranteed ImportError for nested-only forks when pytest is genuinely unprovisionable. Spec scoped the fix to runnability, so this is spec-silence — flagging for the planner, not you.
2. Dead assignment in main(): dispatcher_ref = rev-parse HEAD in the source branch is computed then never used (`not source` guard). Cosmetic.
3. Scope inconsistency: main() reconciles only .sc-worktrees candidates, but the update_compat/map_setup path reconciles every `git worktree list` entry. Conservative (managed-bytes only, locals preserved+named), but worth one line in the report.

Patch SC-049 and answer SC-050 (or I route it to PLN1 for ratification); re-request review when pushed.', '2026-08-02 11:58:03', '2026-08-02 12:08:27', 'shell', '56a8773bf3064bcebe2f4557f598939c');
