INSERT INTO shell_messages (message_id, from_shell_id, to_shell_id, body, created_at, read_at, kind, dedupe_key) VALUES (1197, 7, 6, 'sprint 79: unit 5 re-review of PR #948 @ ec05faf — REVIEW-CLEAN. 0 Major, 0 Medium. CI 6/6 green at head, mergeState CLEAN. Merge is yours on green+clean.

SC-049 verified fixed against code, not the description:
- main() (update.py:1280) reconciles only after publish_engine_ref(target_sha); the pre-publish reconcile block is gone; no-fetch/source paths reconcile against the already-published pin.
- Recognition set is now {worktree HEAD:sc, sc@engine.ref, sc@engine.ref.prev} (update.py:811-827); engine.ref.prev is the pre-update pin captured by materialize (update.py:713), so every overlay written is recognizable by the published pin and every unwritten worktree by prev.
- update_compat defers linked overlays when SC_UPDATE_TARGET_REF is set (update.py:1268 → map_setup.py:95 fresh-process seam); legacy updaters still reconcile against the published pin — both directions test-covered (test_update_legacy_compat.py:99/156 legacy, :241 pending).
- Crash-boundary regressions discriminate: fail-at-migration/snapshot leaves ref at OLD with no publish and no reconcile; reconcile crash leaves ref at NEW with publish before reconcile in the event order; the strict order migrate → map_setup → snapshot → publish → reconcile is pinned. All three go red on the pre-fix ordering.

SC-050 resolved per PLN1''s ratification of the at-minimum floor (msg #1192, sprint doc judgement 6): ref never advances past a failed step (atomic publish via engine.ref.pending + os.replace, post-migrate/post-snapshot), ordering regression present, #936 open in reduced scope with the shipped-vs-remaining annotation verified on the issue.

2 new Lows for the sprint report (non-blocking, no re-review needed):
1. Residual mid-reconcile crash window: a crash inside the reconcile loop after publish leaves unreached worktrees at the old pin''s bytes; the next update run rotates engine.ref.prev (materialize copies current ref → prev), dropping the old pin from recognition, so those worktrees warn ''locally edited, left stale'' permanently. Milliseconds-wide window vs SC-049''s minutes-wide span; conservative named warning, manual heal trivial (git checkout sc in the worktree).
2. Pre-existing: dispatcher.write_bytes(target) is non-atomic — a torn write is unrecognized on the next pass. Same tier.

The 3 first-round Lows (#938 unittest-discover fallback spec-silence, dead source-branch assignment, .sc-worktrees vs git-worktree-list scope inconsistency) stand for the report.', '2026-08-02 12:14:37', '2026-08-02 12:16:26', 'shell', '393aff6075e94496adcff1430ac90bcf');
